export const COMPOSITION_GUIDE_VERSION = 1;

/**
 * This text is intentionally generated by the architecture package. It is the
 * adapter contract between a handoff and the independently installed
 * html-design skill; it is not a copy of that skill.
 */
export function buildCompositionGuide({ archiePage = null } = {}) {
  const archieMarkerInstruction = archiePage
    ? `- When \`page-map.json\` includes the Archie area, preserve all \`archie-*\` comments from \`${archiePage.markdown}\` in the composed \`site/${archiePage.slug}/index.html\`. Keep them as HTML comments so they remain hidden while the final-site check can read the guide contract.\n`
    : "";
  return `# Architecture handoff composition guide

This handoff is the architecture source bundle for the independently installed \`html-design\` skill. Use the handoff as the only architecture-content input. Do not import the architecture builder, its source files, or its dependencies into the final site.

## Ownership and lifecycle

- \`handoff/\` is the authoritative architecture input bundle.
- \`preview/\` is a disposable builder-owned regression preview; it is not the final presentation.
- \`site/\` is owned and published by the html-design consumer. An architecture rebuild must never replace it.
- Start each update by reading \`delta.json\` and \`delta.md\`, then inspect the affected claims, pages, and view IDs.
- Treat \`claims.json\` and \`page-map.json\` as the curated architecture contract. Treat copied Markdown as authored narrative. Presentation may add structure and styling, but must not alter claims, review status, evidence links, page intent, or view identity.
${archieMarkerInstruction}- After composition, write \`site/assets/architecture-handoff.json\` with the current \`manifest.digests.handoff\`, \`manifest.version\`, and an HTML-design ownership marker. This receipt lets the architecture checker detect a presentation composed from an older handoff.

## Profile and page composition

Before composing, treat each page record as a rendering contract. Render every listed view on that page, show its initialViewId first, and keep the page's view selector scoped to those view IDs. For the final-site checks, declare those IDs as a comma-separated data-view-ids value on the body, declare data-initial-view, mount that initial view, and provide a page-local select#view-select. Do not leave a page with declared views but no mounted diagram.

Use the html-design \`rail-document\` profile. A composed page should have this shape:

1. one page-level \`<main>\` and one page-level \`<h1>\`; the page-map title supplies that sole h1;
2. a skip link before repeated navigation and a labelled page rail or equivalent navigation;
3. a statement-led introduction using the page title and summary;
4. authored Markdown placed as narrative, preserving decisions, rationale, trade-offs, domain language, and evidence rather than reducing it to cards;
5. relevant diagrams framed by a title, description, caption, and accessible scroll region;
6. claims and evidence presented as provenance, including their review status;
7. a clear next-reading or navigation path.

The Markdown files can contain full CommonMark structure. Use them as content, not as the final visual layout. Authored h1 headings are lowered below the page-map h1 by the architecture preview; the final composition must preserve the same one-h1 rule. Keep headings in order and do not repeat a diagram as prose when the diagram is the relationship explanation.

## Canonical HTML-design assets

Follow the consumer skill's current asset contract. Load the current marked theme asset, exactly one named palette, and the canonical design-system stylesheet. Use the \`rail-document\` profile and its current patterns; do not copy a palette from this bundle or invent source-specific design-system names. Keep all CSS, JavaScript, fonts, images, and other resources local to the final artifact. Evidence hyperlinks are links, not remotely loaded assets.

If an evidence link is intentionally external, put \`data-ds-check-ignore="external-request" data-ds-check-reason="External evidence link; it is navigated by the reader and is not a remotely loaded artifact resource."\` on that link. Do not use that suppression for scripts, stylesheets, fonts, images, or other resource requests.

## Handoff files and relative paths

Use these exact paths relative to the handoff directory:

- \`manifest.json\` — schema, ownership, counts, pinned compiler version, input digests, and the handoff digest.
- \`composition-guide.md\` — this generated adapter contract.
- \`claims.json\` — evidence-backed claims and review state.
- \`page-map.json\` — ordered pages, summaries, Markdown paths, claim IDs, view IDs, and each page's initial view ID.
- \`pages/<page-id>.md\` — copied authored Markdown.
- \`assets/views.json\` — view metadata, parent navigation, and stable semantic digests.
- \`assets/likec4-views.js\` — the compiled LikeC4 web component bundle.
- \`delta.json\` and \`delta.md\` — baseline/update changes for incremental recomposition.

When a final page is nested below \`site/\`, resolve handoff-derived resources into the final site's own copied \`assets/\` directory. Do not use absolute filesystem paths. Keep route-relative paths correct for every page depth.

## LikeC4 mounting adapter

Copy \`assets/likec4-views.js\` into the final site's local assets and load it as a module. Use a labelled, keyboard-focusable scroll frame and mount the component with a named view:

\`\`\`html
<div class="ds-scroll-x" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Scrollable architecture diagram">
  <likec4-view view-id="<view-id>" browser="true" color-scheme="light"></likec4-view>
</div>
<script type="module" src="assets/likec4-views.js"></script>
\`\`\`

In the adapter, wait for the element before using it, then render it:

\`\`\`js
await customElements.whenDefined("likec4-view");
const viewer = document.querySelector("likec4-view");
viewer.render();
\`\`\`

Populate a view selector from \`assets/views.json\`. Preserve each view's ID, title, description, group, and optional \`parentId\`. Selecting a view updates \`view-id\`, the title, description, and status. A parent control navigates to \`parentId\`; view controls must remain reachable by keyboard.

Apply these viewer settings because the browser's wheel event otherwise pans instead of zooming:

- \`pannable: true\`
- \`zoomable: true\`
- \`keepAspectRatio: false\`
- \`zoomOnScroll: true\`
- \`panOnScroll: false\`

Set the first three on the component props returned by the viewer adapter, and set the last two in both \`reactFlowProps\` objects (top-level and nested browser options when present). Dragging the diagram pans; the wheel zooms. Keep the wide canvas inside a labelled \`ds-scroll-x\` region rather than allowing the page to overflow horizontally.

## Canvas sizing and captions

Default the diagram stage to width: 100% and min-width: 0 so the first render fits the desktop content area. Add a larger minimum width only for an intentional, keyboard-scrollable narrow-layout view. Verify the initial fit before testing zoom. Keep explanatory captions in a visually separate block below the canvas, not over the diagram surface.

## Accessibility, theme, responsive, and print behavior

- Give every diagram frame a visible title and description; give inline SVG alternatives a \`viewBox\`, \`role="img"\`, and linked \`title\` and \`desc\`.
- Keep the diagram scroll region at \`tabindex="0"\` and make focus visible. A view shortcut should move focus to that region after navigation.
- Keep headings, captions, selectors, and controls outside the horizontally scrolling region. Style captions as separate blocks below the canvas so explanatory prose cannot look like diagram content.
- Support narrow layouts without document-wide overflow; diagrams may scroll inside their labelled region.
- Synchronize document theme with the LikeC4 \`color-scheme\` attribute: explicit light and dark choices win, while system follows \`prefers-color-scheme\`.
- Provide a print fallback. Hide interactive viewer controls and canvas in print, and print a clear caption explaining how to explore the omitted interactive diagram over HTTP.

## Verification

Serve the final site over HTTP, not with file://. The architecture package's npm run serve command serves only the disposable preview. Use python3 -m http.server 4173 --directory site, or the consumer's equivalent, for the final site.

Run the html-design checks from the consumer installation against the composed artifact:

\`\`\`bash
node "$HTML_DESIGN_SKILL_DIR/scripts/check-artifact.mjs" site/index.html --profile rail-document
\`\`\`

Then run the final-site contract and browser checks against every composed route:

\`\`\`bash
node "$PACKAGE_DIR/bin/architecture-docs.mjs" check:site --config architecture-docs.config.json
node "$PACKAGE_DIR/bin/architecture-docs.mjs" check:site:browser --config architecture-docs.config.json
\`\`\`

Inspect desktop, narrow, light, dark, keyboard focus, wheel zoom, drag pan, and print output. The architecture package's \`check:html\` and \`test:browser\` commands validate only \`preview/\`; they do not replace final-site or html-design review. Before publication, run the architecture publication check and ensure the receipt's \`architectureHandoffDigest\` equals \`manifest.digests.handoff\`.
`;
}
